{"body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object. The documented rule I am reading is: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\". The two replies are, first, a javascript-fenced object followed by a bare object: ```javascript\n{\"a\": 1}\n```\n{\"b\": 2}; and second, just the bare object: {\"b\": 2}. For the first reply the rule requires the answer {\"a\": 1}, because the code fence holds an object and it is the last fenced object. For the second reply the rule requires {\"b\": 2}, because no fence holds an object, so the last top-level object is used. The check gives the same answer for both: {\"b\": 2}, because its fence regex only recognizes ```json, ```JSON, or an untagged fence, so the javascript fence is not captured and the later top-level object wins. I would be wrong if recover_json_object returns {\"a\": 1} for the first reply, or if the documented rule is read as applying only to fences tagged json, JSON, or untagged.", "commitments": ""}